# Straightforward construction of hollow polyoxometalate-based metal-organic framework via pseudo-homoepitaxial growth

1Key Laboratory of Polyoxometalate and Reticular Material Chemistry of Ministry of Education, College of Chemistry, Northeast Normal University, Changchun 130024, China; 2Zhang Dayu School of Chemistry, Dalian University of Technology, Dalian 116024, China
Hollow structures are significant for shortening diffusion path and promoting active center exposure. Single-crystalline hollow polyoxometalate-based metal-organic frameworks (POM@MOFs) with increased adsorption performance and catalytic activity were synthesized through a straightforward pseudo-homoepitaxial growth strategy within only $1.5\mathrm{min}$ . The strategy ingeniously exploits favorable characteristics of well-matched lattice/structure but acid stability difference between MOF and POM@MOF to construct a pseudo-homoepitaxial structure. Specifically, the MOF single crystals are used as seeds to perform epitaxial growth of the lattice/structure-matched POM@MOF under appropriate conditions. Interestingly, the MOF seeds collapse spontaneously during epitaxial growth, affording regular hollow POM@MOF single crystals. Because the acid stability of the POM@MOF is much higher than that of the MOF, it is speculated that the hollow structure formation may be related to the acid released from the epitaxial POM@MOF growth. And the pseudo-homoepitaxial POM@MOF growth strategy based on structural matching but stability difference avoids additional template removal process in conventional hollow MOF preparation and ensures hollow single crystal construction.
## polyoxometalate-based metal-organic framework, pseudo-homoepitaxial growth, hollow structure, single crystals, etching
Citation: Zhang Z, Ma X, Han X, Cui H, Lu Y, Liu S, Liu Y. Straightforward construction of hollow polyoxometalate-based metal-organic framework via pseudo-homoepitaxial growth. Sc China Chem, 2022, 65, https://doi.org/10.1007/s11426-022-1295-3
## 1 Introduction
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are crystalline hybrid materials consisting of metal ions (or clusters) and organic ligands linked together through coordination bonds [1]. Because of their high porosity, large surface areas and multiple active centers (metal or organic sites), these solids can serve as excellent acid, base or oxidation catalysts [2–4]. Furthermore, due to their unique supporting effect, transport capacity, and activation performance, MOFs are also outstanding support materials [3,5,6]. Owing to their crystalline nature, MOFs even can support some functional components to construct single-crystalline composite catalysts. Typically, many suitable active polyoxometalates (POMs) can be assembled into MOFs by covalent bonding or supramolecular interaction, thus affording polyoxometalate-based MOFs (POM@MOFs) [7–9]. As a class of unique reticular material with synergistic advantages of POMs and MOFs, $\mathrm{POM}(\mathcal{Q}).$ - MOFs show great promise for a wide application such as catalysis, energy, and environment [9–12].
Recently, hollow MOFs containing enclosed cavities and thin shells have received widespread attention due to their shortened microporous diffusion distance, active site exposure, and the ability to encapsulate active units to construct microreactors [13–17]. Microporous MOF shells play an important role in selective mass transfer, permeation, adsorption, and catalysis, while internal cavity can accommodate active components and prevent their undesirable agglomeration [13,14]. Though a myriad of methods have been developed for constructing hollow MOFs, all of them are essentially template methods [13]. And the templates used can be divided into two categories—heterologous and homologous templates. The main heterologous templates reported include polystyrene spheres [18–20], metal oxides [21–23], microemulsions [24–26] , and typical homologous templates are self-templates [27,28] or isomorphic MOF templates [29–32].
The usage of heterogeneous templates inevitably suffers from some trouble. For instance they need to be large enough to form hollow cavity but are not easy to remove [18,23]. In addition, the structural mismatch (e.g., composition and lattice) between heterologous templates and target MOFs usually results in polycrystalline shells with poor stability and compactness [18–26]. When the homologous templates are used, it is necessary to engineer stability (for self-templates) [27,28] or composition (for isomorphic MOF templates) [29,30] heterogeneity, and then remove labile parts or components to obtain hollow motifs. The advantage of isomorphic MOF templates lies in ease of heterogeneous singlecrystalline construction, which mainly involves the wellknown ZIF-8 and ZIF-67 (the isostructural MOFs built from $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}/\mathrm{Co}^{2+}$ and 2-methylimidazole) MOF pair [29–31]. Although they hold identical crystal structure, the metal nodes and thus the coordination bond strength are different [29– 31]. The removal of homologous templates essentially depends on an etching process, which is actually in favor of fabricating hollow single crystals [27,31,32]. Compared with polycrystalline structures, hierarchical single crystals tend to have higher stability and molecular selectivity because of the only lattice [31]. At present, constructing hollow MOFs via various templates mostly requires appropriate epitaxial growth and subsequent additional template removal process, which leads to complicated procedure and high cost. Although researchers have tried to use the isomorphic ZIF-8 and ZIF-67 pair to directly construct hollow MOF single crystals, they often involve complex and harsh conditions such as prolonged high-temperature solvothermal [29,33], and ultrasound [34]. Moreover, the residual Co-rich species are easy to form inside the cavity [29,33]. To this end, fabricating hollow single-crystalline MOFs through a simple straightforward procedure without any additional processing remains challenging under mild conditions.
Considering the functional merits of POM@MOFs, such as increased catalytic and adsorption active centers, it is significant for constructing hollow POM@MOFs to optimize their performance. However, both the complicated synthesis system of POM $@$ MOFs and the high stability caused by inorganic-organic hybrid increase the hollow construction difficulty [8,35]. Herein, we construct hollow POM@MOF single crystals in a short time (only $1.5\mathrm{min}\$ ) by direct pseudo-homoepitaxial growth (PHG) (Figure 1), and no additional template removal process is involved. The strategy ingeniously exploits favorable characteristics of well-matched lattice/structure but acid stability difference between MOF and POM@MOF to construct a pseudohomoepitaxial structure. Interestingly, the MOF seeds collapse spontaneously during epitaxial growth, affording regular hollow POM@MOF single crystals. Considering the acid instability of MOF seeds, we speculate that the formation of the hollow structure may originate from the in-diffusion etching (IDE) of the by-product acid released from the epitaxial assembly of POM $@$ MOF. The process is mild and straightforward, avoiding additional template removal procedure in conventional hollow MOF preparation and ensuring hollow single-crystalline construction. Moreover, the direct formation of hollow structure is also closely related to the enrichment action of POM components on the by-product acid, which is capable of initiating immediate in-diffusion etching process. These indicate that exploiting the dominant roles of POM components has positive and unique effect for the construction of hierarchical POM@MOFs. Therefore, by rationally designing the MOF interface structure and controlling the acid release (such as strength, concentration, rate), it may become a new direct strategy to construct hollow structures.
## 2 Experimental
## 2.1 Procedure for preparation of monodispersed $\vert\mathbf{Cu}_{3}(\mathbf{BTC})_{2}\vert$
Solution A. At $50^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , $\mathrm{Cu(NO_{3})_{2}}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (41 mg, 0.17 mmol) and lauric acid $(1.43~\mathrm{g},7.13~\mathrm{mmol})$ were added to $9\mathrm{mL}$ of ethanol to form a clear solution after stirring for $30\mathrm{min}$ and vacuum filtration.
Solution B. Ground 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylic acid $\mathrm{(H_{3}B T C}$ , $20~\mathrm{mg}$ , $0.095\mathrm{mmol}_{.}$ was dissolved to $1\mathrm{mL}$ of ethanol.
The Solution B was added into Solution A under stirring, and then the clear solution was maintained at $50^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $0.5\mathrm{h}$ under static condition. The crystalline products ( $\mathrm{[Cu}_{3}$ $\mathrm{(BTC)}_{2}]\mathrm{)}$ ) were collected by centrifugation, washed with anhydrous ethanol for three times and were dried at $50^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight.
## 2.2 Procedure for preparation of NENU-3a
Solution C. At ambient temperature $({\sim}25^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ , $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}3$ $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ $(0.4\ \mathrm{g},\ 1.68\ \mathrm{mmol})$ and $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{PW}_{12}\mathrm{O}_{40}$ (HPW) $(0.48~\mathrm{g}.$ ， $0.16\mathrm{mmol}$ ) were added to the mixed solvent of methanol/ chloroform $3\ \mathrm{mL}/12\ \mathrm{mL}$ ) to form a homogenous solution after stirring for $20\mathrm{min}$ and centrifugation $(14,000~\mathrm{r/min}$ , $3\mathrm{min}\dot{}$ ).
Solution D. $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}$ $(0.08\mathrm{~g},\ 0.37\mathrm{mmol})$ was also dissolved into the above mixed solvent to form another homogenous solution.
The $3\mathrm{mL}$ of Solution D with an addition of $400~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of methanol was added into $3~\mathrm{mL}$ of Solution C under stirring, and then the clear solution was maintained at $50^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $10\mathrm{min}$ under static condition. The crystalline products (NENU-3a) were collected by centrifugation, washed with anhydrous ethanol for two times and were dried at $50^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight.
## 2.3 Procedure for preparation of hollow POM@MOFs
Solution E. At ambient temperature $(\sim25^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ , $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}3$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $(0.4\mathrm{g},1.68\mathrm{mmol})$ ) and HPW $\left(0.48\mathrm{~g},0.16\mathrm{mmol}\right)$ were added to the mixed solution of methanol/chloroform $(3~\mathrm{mL}/\$ $7\mathrm{mL}$ ) to form a homogenous solution after stirring for $20\mathrm{min}$ and centrifugation $(14000\ \mathrm{rpm},3\ \mathrm{min})$ .
Suspension F. $5\mathrm{mg}$ as-prepared $\mathrm{[Cu_{3}(B T C)_{2}]}$ was dispersed into $1\mathrm{mL}$ of chloroform by sonication.
First, the Suspension F was added into $2{\mathrm{~mL}}$ Solution E under stirring. Then, the $3~\mathrm{mL}$ of Solution D with an addition of $400\upmu\mathrm{L}$ MeOH was added into above suspension under stirring and then was maintained at $50^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under static condition (0.5 min, 1.0 min, $1.5\mathrm{min}$ or $2~\mathrm{min}$ ). The crystalline products were collected by centrifugation, washed with anhydrous ethanol for two times and were dried at $50^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight.
## 3 Results and discussion
To elaborate the pseudo-homoepitaxial growth strategy, we take the MOFs— $\mathrm{[Cu_{3}(B T C)_{2}]}$ $\mathrm{BTC}=$ trimesic acid anion) and POM@MOF—NENU-3a with perfect matching lattice/ structure but different acid stability as examples. Specifically, the monodispersed $\mathrm{[Cu_{3}(B T C)_{2}]}$ single crystals are used as seeds to perform the epitaxial NENU-3a growth. Simultaneously, the acid-labile $\mathrm{[Cu_{3}(B T C)_{2}]}$ seeds are etched by the by-product acid, thereby resulting in the hollow NENU-3a single crystals (Figure 1). $\mathrm{[Cu_{3}B T C_{2}]}$ is a classic MOF formed by paddle-wheel-structured $\mathrm{Cu(II)}$ dimers bridged with BTC [36], and NENU-3a is built up from $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{PW}_{12}\mathrm{O}_{40}$ (HPW) guest perfectly encapsulated in the $\mathrm{[Cu_{3}B T C_{2}]}$ host through supramolecular assembly (Figure S1, Supporting Information online) [8]. Therefore, they hold the same space group, chemical connections and nearly identical lattice parameters (Figure S1), which are prerequisites for single-crystalline heteroepitaxial growth of NENU-3a on $\mathrm{[Cu_{3}(B T C)_{2}]}$ . This highly matched heteroepitaxial growth can be denoted as PHG. However, to achieve the single-crystalline heteroepitaxial growth, there are two experimental conditions that need to be met. (1) The size and morphology of the $\mathrm{[Cu_{3}(B T C)_{2}]}$ seeds are highly uniform. (2) The epitaxial assembly rate of the NENU-3a shell is appropriate.
Herein, we successfully prepared cuboctahedral $\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\right]$ $\mathrm{BTC}_{2}]$ (ca. $1\upmu\mathrm{m})$ single crystals with highly uniform size and morphology by using lauric acid modulator. The lauric acid achieves a suitable nucleation and growth rate through coordination modulation, resulting in uniform size and morphology (Figure S2) [37]. According to our previous report, the double solvent of chloroform/methanol can coordinate the nucleation and growth of NENU-3a, affording monodispersed NENU-3a octahedrons (ca. $1.8\upmu\mathrm{m}$ , Figure S3) [38]. And the transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images also show the cuboctahedral contour of $\mathrm{[Cu_{3}(B T C)_{2}]}$ and the octahedral appearance of NENU-3a (Figure S4). To this end, we artificially use a mixed solvent of chloroform/ methanol as the epitaxial growth medium of NENU-3a. As shown in scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images, the morphology of obtained epitaxially growing particles obviously transits from cuboctahedron to octahedron (Figure 2, Figures S5 and S6), which indicates that NENU-3a grows faster along ${<}100>$ crystal direction outside $\mathrm{[Cu_{3}(B T C)_{2}]}$ , consistent with the spontaneous octahedral morphology of NENU-3a (Figure S3). In addition, the single regular morphology indicates the epitaxial growth uniformity of NENU3a. However, with the extension of reaction time $(2\:\mathrm{min})$ , the generated particles precipitate by gravity, so the homogeneous nucleation starts in the upper solution, resulting in some small octahedral NENU-3a (Figure S5D). Therefore, the optimized longest reaction time is $1.5\mathrm{min}$ . The size of epitaxial grown particles gradually increases with time (Figure 2). At $1.5\mathrm{min}$ , the particle size reaches ca. $1.4\upmu\mathrm{m}$ From the simulated powder X-ray diffraction (XRD) spectrum (Figure 3a), it is not difficult to find that (111) $(5.9^{\circ})$ and (220) $(9.5^{\circ})$ are the characteristic intensity diffraction peaks of NENU-3a and $\mathrm{[Cu_{3}(B T C)_{2}]}$ (related to the crystal structure [39]), respectively. Compared with the simulated and synthesized XRD patterns of $\mathrm{[Cu_{3}(B T C)_{2}]}$ , the $\{111\}$ crystal plane diffraction peak evidently appears on the XRD patterns of epitaxial grown particles (Figure 3a). And as reaction time prolonged, the peak intensity of the $\{111\}$ crystal facet increased (Figure 3a). In addition, the absorption peaks at 1,078, 982, 890, and $827~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ on the FT-IR spectrum correspond to $\mathrm{\DeltaP-O_{a}}$ , $\mathrm{W=O_{d}},$ $\mathrm{W{-}O_{\mathrm{b}}{-}W}$ and $\mathrm{\DeltaW-O_{c}-}$ W vibration of HPW (Figure 3b and Figure S7), respectively. The related characteristic peaks on XRD patterns and FT-IR spectrums confirm the existence of NENU-3a component, $i$ e., the successful epitaxial growth. Moreover, the content of NENU-3a also increased over time (Figure 3).
In order to further explore the internal/interface structure of the epitaxially growing particles, TEM measurements were conducted (Figure 4). Surprisingly, the crystalline particle interior collapsed obviously, affording an internal mesoporous structure $(0.5\mathrm{min})$ (Figure 4b and Figure S8A). As time prolonged, the internal collapse became more obvious (Figure 4c and Figure S8B), and finally a clear hollow structure (Figure 4d and Figure S8C) formed at $1.5\mathrm{min}$ . The cavity size is about $700\mathrm{nm}$ , and none of residual species remains in the cavity. In addition, the clear void of the broken particle also confirms the hollow formation (Figure S5C). The distribution of $\mathrm{Cu}$ on the energy dispersive X-ray (EDX) elemental mapping further highlights the hollow structure, while the distribution of W suggests that HPW, i.e., NENU3a evenly exists on the particle exterior (Figure 4e), which again confirms the uniform epitaxial growth of NENU-3a. Previous studies have shown that $\mathrm{[Cu_{3}(B T C)_{2}]}$ is more acidlabile than NENU-3a [8,38,40]. However, $\mathrm{[Cu_{3}(B T C)_{2}]}$ is stable in the $\mathrm{HPW/Cu(NO_{3})_{2}}$ solution (Figure S9), which should be attributed to the fact that the HPW dissociation is inhibited in the weak polar mixture of chloroform/methanol [41], and thus both free $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}^{+}$ or ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ are less. Therefore, it can be concluded that the internal structure collapse (i.e., etching) is not caused by HPW or ${\mathrm{NO}}_{3}^{-}$ . Additionally, both $\mathrm{[Cu_{3}(B T C)_{2}]}$ and NENU-3a were not etched during their self-assembly process (homoepitaxial growth) to form hollow cavity (Figure S4). And it is thus clear that the hollow structure formation is related to the MOF pair, and is not caused by a single component.
On the basis of the above results and the stability difference of the MOF pair, we propose an in-diffusion etching mechanism (Figure 1). In other words, we attributed the hollow structure to the in-diffusion etching caused by ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ from NENU-3a assembly (Figure 1). Specifically, during the epitaxial growth of NENU-3a on $\mathrm{[Cu_{3}(B T C)_{2}]}$ , ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ is released from the reactions between $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}$ and $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}$ (Figure S10). The generated ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ are enriched around the HPW in the confined space through hydrogen bonding (Figure 5a). With further epitaxial growth, ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ molecules diffuse inwardly driven by the concentration difference, and then etch the acid-labile $\mathrm{[Cu_{3}(B T C)_{2}]}$ cores (Figure 5a). Although ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ molecules are also generated during the assembly of $\mathrm{[Cu_{3}(B T C)_{2}]}$ (Figure S10), only solid particles form (Figure S4A). This may be because the generated ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ without the hydrogen bond binding directly diffuses the external solution environment, and the $\mathrm{[Cu_{3}(B T C)_{2}]}$ inside is not etched by the ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ with reduced concentration (Figure 5b). It is worth mentioning that despite the presence of HPW, no hollow cavity forms during the self-assembly of
NENU-3a because of its higher stability (Figure S4B). As TEM images show, the etching actually starts from the inside core of each particle, and gradually extends outwards, finally forming a hollow cavity (Figure 4 and Figure S8). According to previous reports, during MOF nucleation and initial growth stages, more defects form inside core than outer layer because of high supersaturation as well as fast reaction rate, and therefore the core is preferentially etched [27,28,32]. The hollow formation starting from the interior is a typical feature of the in-diffusion of the small molecule etchant [27,28,32], which is different from the structural transformation beginning with the surface or the interface [33,42].
To further confirm the proposed internal diffusion mechanism, $\mathrm{[Cu_{3}(B T C)_{2}]}$ was purposely etched by ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ with different concentrations. The concentration of ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ (ca. $4.7\mathrm{mM}$ , based on total solvent volume) generated during the epitaxial growth can be calculated from the particle yield $(1.5\mathrm{min})$ and corresponding NENU-3a content, and the concentration is denoted as $C_{0}$ . However, $\mathrm{[Cu_{3}(B T C)_{2}]}$ was stable under the nitric acid concentration of $C_{0},$ and neither the outside nor the inside of the particle was etched, which is manifested by the smooth surface and the absence of defects inside (Figure S11A, C). However, when the ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ concentration was increased to $15C_{0}.$ , the $\mathrm{[Cu_{3}(B T C)_{2}]}$ surface became rough, indicating the occurrence of the etching reaction (Figure S11B). Interestingly, the inner part of $\left[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}\right]$ $\left(\mathrm{BTC}\right)_{2}]$ was more obviously etched (Figure S11D). It is thus clear that the particle interior is indeed easier to be etched compared with the outer layer, which is consistent with the phenomenon and discussion mentioned above. When the ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ concentration was further increased, $\mathrm{[Cu_{3}(B T C)_{2}]}$ was completely decomposed (Figure S11E). According to this series of etching experiments, $\mathrm{[Cu_{3}(B T C)_{2}]}$ can be significantly etched by the “high-concentration” ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ , but is stable in the low-concentration one. Therefore, it also confirms the enrichment effect of the outer NENU-3a on the ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ generated during the epitaxial growth. And then the in-diffusion of the “high-concentration” ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ in the confined space triggers the engraving of $\mathrm{[Cu_{3}(B T C)_{2}]}$ cores.
As described above, the concentration of ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ generated from the epitaxial growth is ca. $4.7\mathrm{mM}$ , i.e., $0.03\mathrm{mmol}$ . And this ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ concentration cannot actually cause the etching of $\mathrm{[Cu_{3}(B T C)_{2}]}$ (Figure S11). However, the enriched ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ caused by the HPW in microporous NENU-3a triggers the in-diffusion engraving of $\mathrm{[Cu_{3}(B T C)_{2}]}$ cores (Figure 5a). It is worth noting that the ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ amount consumed for the complete decomposition of $\mathrm{[Cu_{3}(B T C)_{2}]}$ used $(5~\mathrm{mg})$ is $0.05\mathrm{mmol}$ in theory. Therefore, despite the enriched ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ , the acid amount from epitaxial growth is not enough to completely remove $\mathrm{[Cu_{3}(B T C)_{2}]}$ cores, thus explaining the partial residue of $\mathrm{[Cu_{3}(B T C)_{2}]}$ . But fortunately, these do not affect the hollow cavity formation due to the intrinsic stability gradient in $\mathrm{[Cu_{3}(B T C)_{2}]}$ as discussed in previous reports, i.e., the low stability as well as ease of etching of the interior [27,28,32].
With the pseudo-homoepitaxial growth strategy, we also prepared nanosized hollow NENU-3a. Firstly, we successfully prepared nanoscaled $\mathrm{[Cu_{3}(B T C)_{2}]}$ $(\sim650\mathrm{nm})$ by shortening crystallization time ( $\mathrm{{10}m i n)}$ (Figure S13). Then, the hollow NENU-3a $(\sim840\mathrm{nm})$ with nanometer size was obtained by the epitaxial growth under the same conditions (Figures S14 and S15), which also demonstrates that the epitaxial growth strategy has a certain flexibility. Moreover, the hollow structure construction methodology can be extended to another $\scriptstyle\mathrm{POM}\ @\mathrm{MOF}$ , namely NENU-5a ( $\mathrm{[Cu}_{12}$ $\mathrm{(BTC)_{8}][H_{3}P M o_{12}O_{40}])}$ (Figure S16). Hollow NENU-5a shows similar morphology, cavity and crystallinity with those of hollow NENU-3a (Figures S16). This potential universality suggests that the proposed approach may work well in other $\mathrm{POM}\ @\mathrm{MOF}$ systems.
Sulfur mustard, i.e., bis(2-chloroethyl)sulfide, is a highly toxic chemical warfare agent that can cause skin blistering, erosion, and even death [44,45]. Unfortunately, mustard gas has no specific anti-toxic drugs so far. Therefore, the efficient detoxification of mustard gas has attracted much attention. Although dehalogenation and hydrolysis can detoxify mustard gas, they suffer from slow reaction kinetics and incomplete reactions [44,45]. A promising detoxification method is selective oxidation to the sulfoxide product, which crucially requires a suitable catalyst because the over-oxidative sulfone product is nearly as toxic as mustard itself [44]. Considering the thinned shell of the hollow structure and the reversible redox property of HPW [46,47], we explored the catalytic oxidation performance of hollow NENU3a towards mustard gas simulant (2-chloroethyl ethyl sulfide, CEES) (Figure 6a). With $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ as the oxidant, the hollow NENU-3a exhibits faster catalytic reaction kinetics than the solid one (Figure S17). The substrate CEES was almost completely oxidized within 30 min with hollow NENU-3a as catalyst, while the CEES conversion catalyzed by solid NENU-3a was only $86\%$ (Figure 6a). The faster reaction kinetics of hollow NENU-3a should be because the thinned shells effectively shorten the microporous diffusion distance and facilitate the accessibility to active centers.
According to previous reports, the active center for catalytic CEES oxidation is mainly HPW [35,48]. Therefore, the better catalytic performance of hollow NENU-3a indicates that the buried active center in solid one because of prolonged microporous channels and poor accessibility weakens the catalysis exertion. Meaningfully, hollow NENU-3a exhibits higher sulfoxide product (CEESO) selectivity than solid NENU-3a (Figure 6b and Figure S18), which profits from the rapid departure of the intermediate CEESO from the thinned shell structure, weakening its over oxidation. And then the hollow NENU-3a catalyst was recycled via simple centrifugation and used in next run of CEES oxidation. The catalytic activity of hollow NENU-3a did not decrease significantly, as evidenced by the basically constant conversion, selectivity and kinetics (Figure 6c and Figure S19). The SEM and TEM images of the recycled hollow NENU-3a show maintained morphology and cavity structure (Figure S20A, B), indicating the good mechanical stability under solution and stirring environment. And the crystallinity of the recycled hollow NENU-3a catalyst is also well-maintained (Figure S20C). The above results all proves the good cycling stability of hollow NENU-3a single crystals.
## 4 Conclusions
In summary, we developed a simple and straightforward pseudo-homoepitaxial growth strategy to successfully construct hollow POM@MOFs in a short time $(1.5\mathrm{min})$ under mild conditions. The monodispersed MOF single crystals are used as seeds to perform the epitaxial growth of $\mathrm{POM}(\mathcal{Q}).$ - MOF with well-matched lattice/structure but significant stability difference. During this process, the single-crystalline seeds, well-matched lattice/structure, and suitable assembly conditions enable the uniform pseudo-homoepitaxial growth of POM@MOF. Meanwhile, the internal MOF seed crystals collapse spontaneously, and the hollow POM@MOF single crystals with regular morphology directly form. Since the acid stability of MOF seeds is significantly weaker than that of POM@MOF, it is speculated that the hollow structure may originate from the etching on the inner MOF cores by the by-product acid generated from the epitaxial $\mathrm{POM}(\mathcal{O}).$ - MOF growth. The ingenious epitaxial growth based on structural matching but stability difference avoids additional template removal process in conventional hollow MOF construction, which provides a new strategic choice for the design of hollow POM@MOF single crystals. Moreover, the hollow structure is also of great significance for shortening the microporous diffusion distance and avoiding the waste of buried active centers. In addition, some functional components such as alkaline particles and precious metal nanocrystals may be introduced into the MOF cores, and corresponding yolk-shell composites may be constructed via the straightforward epitaxial growth, which may further serve as catalysts for some acid-base or acid-oxidation cascade reactions.
